{:edb/db-before-t 2196 :edb/db-after-t 2197 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535509 50 #inst "2026-10-09T01:30:43.320Z" 13194139535509 true] [17592186087512 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066605/assets/658fa5a494ef01b5924847335ce863aa8c6b5f8adee5ee97967a8c8bebd83a80.png)\n\nThe Argand diagram above shows the roots of a quartic polynomial $f(x).$ Find $f(x)$ given that the coefficient of the quartic term is $1.$" 13194139535509 true] [17592186087512 1100 "The Argand diagram above shows the roots of a quartic polynomial $f(x)$. Find $f(x)$ given that the coefficient of the quartic term is $1$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/897/Source/Images/0dcad03e8bddad5bbf3557260060a9c4.png)" 13194139535509 false] [17592186087512 1145 "From the diagram, we see that $f(x)$ has roots ${x_1=-2}$, ${x_2=3}$, and\n\n$$\n{x_{3,4}=1 \\pm 3\\textrm{i}}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given that the coefficient of the quartic term is ${a=1}$. Therefore, we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= a(x - x_{1})(x - x_{2})(x - x_{3})(x - x_{4}) \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot (x - (-2))(x - (3))(x - (1 + 3\\textrm{i}))(x - (1 - 3\\textrm{i})) \\\\\n&= (x + 2)(x - 3)(x - (1 + 3\\textrm{i}))(x - (1 - 3\\textrm{i}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 2)(x - 3)(x - (1 + 3\\textrm{i}))(x - (1 - 3\\textrm{i})) &=  \\\\\n(x^{2} - 3x + 2x - 6)(x^{2} - x(1 - 3\\textrm{i}) - x(1 + 3\\textrm{i}) + (1 + 3\\textrm{i})(1 - 3\\textrm{i})) &=  \\\\\n(x^{2} - x - 6)(x^{2} - 2x + 10) &=  \\\\\nx^{4} - 2x^{3} + 10x^{2} - x^{3} + 2x^{2} - 10x - 6x^{2} + 12x - 60 &=  \\\\\n&= x^{4} - 3x^{3} + 6x^{2} + 2x - 60 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^4 - 3x^3 + 6x^2 + 2x - 60\n$$" 13194139535509 false] [17592186087512 1145 "From the diagram, we see that $f(x)$ has roots ${x_1=-2},$ ${x_2=3},$ and ${x_{3,4}=1 \\pm 3\\textrm{i}}.$ Additionally, we're given that the coefficient of the quartic term is ${a=1}.$ Therefore, we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3})(x-{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (x-(-2))(x-(3))(x-(1+3\\text{i}))(x-(1-3\\text{i})) \\\\  & =(x+2)(x-3)(x-(1+3\\text{i}))(x-(1-3\\text{i}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)(x-3)(x-(1+3\\text{i}))(x-(1-3\\text{i})) & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}-3x+2x-6)({x}^{2}-x(1-3\\text{i})-x(1+3\\text{i})+(1+3\\text{i})(1-3\\text{i})) & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}-x-6)({x}^{2}-2x+10) & = \\\\ {x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-10x-6{x}^{2}+12x-60 & = \\\\ {x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+2x-60 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x) = x^4 - 3x^3 + 6x^2 + 2x - 60.$" 13194139535509 true] [17592186087587 1100 "The quartic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots $x_1=0, x_2=-1,x_3=2$ and $x_4=-3$, and the coefficient of the quartic term is $4$. Find $P(x)$." 13194139535509 false] [17592186087587 1100 "The quartic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots $x_1=0, x_2=-1,x_3=2$ and $x_4=-3,$ and the coefficient of the quartic term is $4.$ Find $P(x).$" 13194139535509 true] [17592186087587 1145 "Since our quartic polynomial has roots\n\n$$\n{x_1=0, x_2=-1}\n$$\n\n${x_3=2}$, and ${x_4=-3}$, and the coefficient of the quartic term is ${a=4}$, we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na(x - x_{1})(x - x_{2})(x - x_{3})(x - x_{4}) &= 4(x - 0)(x - (-1))(x - 2)(x - (-3)) \\\\\n&= 4(x - 0)(x + 1)(x - 2)(x + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4(x - 0)(x + 1)(x - 2)(x + 3) &= 4(x^{2} + x)(x^{2} + x - 6) \\\\\n&= 4(x^{4} + x^{3} - 6x^{2} + x^{3} + x^{2} - 6x) \\\\\n&= 4(x^{4} + 2x^{3} - 5x^{2} - 6x) \\\\\n&= 4x^{4} + 8x^{3} - 20x^{2} - 24x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(x) = 4x^4+8x^3-20x^2-24x\n$$" 13194139535509 false] [17592186087587 1145 "Since our quartic polynomial has roots ${x_1=0, x_2=-1},$ ${x_3=2},$ and ${x_4=-3},$ and the coefficient of the quartic term is ${a=4},$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3})(x-{x}_{4}) & =4(x-0)(x-(-1))(x-2)(x-(-3)) \\\\  & =4(x-0)(x+1)(x-2)(x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-0)(x+1)(x-2)(x+3) & =4({x}^{2}+x)({x}^{2}+x-6) \\\\  & =4({x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-6x) \\\\  & =4({x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}-6x) \\\\  & =4{x}^{4}+8{x}^{3}-20{x}^{2}-24x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x) = 4x^4+8x^3-20x^2-24x.$" 13194139535509 true] [17592186087698 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352819 13194139535509 true] [17592186087698 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352833 13194139535509 true] [17592186087698 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352837 13194139535509 true] [17592186087698 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352840 13194139535509 true] [17592186087754 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352805 13194139535509 true] [17592186087754 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352812 13194139535509 true] [17592186087754 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352823 13194139535509 true] [17592186087754 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352827 13194139535509 true] [17592186087754 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352830 13194139535509 true] [17592186088345 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535509 true] [17592186088345 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066605/assets/07724ed5ffd4843ab0a23b0029eeec6291ae698aba71f56f99fb7ac565287932.png)\n\nThe Argand diagram above shows the roots of a quartic polynomial $f(x).$ Find $f(x)$ given that the coefficient of the quartic term is $1.$" 13194139535509 true] [17592186088345 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/897/Source/Images/q-8210.png)\n\nThe Argand diagram above shows the roots of a quartic polynomial $f(x)$. Find $f(x)$ given that the coefficient of the quartic term is $1$." 13194139535509 false] [17592186088345 1145 "From the diagram, we see that $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=-1,$ ${x}_{2}=4,$ and ${x}_{3,4}=\\pm 2\\text{i}.$ Additionally, we're given that the coefficient of the quartic term is $a=1.$ Therefore, we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3})(x-{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =1(x-(-1))(x-4)(x-(-2\\text{i}))(x-(2\\text{i})) \\\\  & =1(x+1)(x-4)(x+2\\text{i})(x-2\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)(x-4)(x+2\\text{i})(x-2\\text{i}) & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}+x-4x-4)({x}^{2}+(2\\text{i}-2\\text{i})x-(2\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}-3x-4)({x}^{2}+4) & = \\\\ {x}^{4}+4{x}^{2}-3{x}^{3}-12x-4{x}^{2}-16 & = \\\\ {x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-12x-16 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-12x-16.$" 13194139535509 true] [17592186352805 1017 #edb/ref 17592186352806 13194139535509 true] [17592186352805 1089 "q-105748" 13194139535509 true] [17592186352805 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535509 true] [17592186352805 1100 "The quartic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=2,$ and the coefficient of the quartic term is $2.$ Find $P(x).$" 13194139535509 true] [17592186352805 1113 #uuid "278ce71d-00cf-5c20-9289-38eadcd1be8f" 13194139535509 true] [17592186352805 1145 "Since our quartic polynomial has roots ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=2,$ and the coefficient of the quartic term is $a=2,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3})(x-{x}_{4}) & =2(x-2)(x-2)(x-2)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x-2)(x-2)(x-2)(x-2) & =2({x}^{2}-4x+4)({x}^{2}-4x+4) \\\\  & =2({x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3}+16{x}^{2}-16x+4{x}^{2}-16x+16) \\\\  & =2({x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+24{x}^{2}-32x+16) \\\\  & =2{x}^{4}-16{x}^{3}+48{x}^{2}-64x+32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=2{x}^{4}-16{x}^{3}+48{x}^{2}-64x+32.$" 13194139535509 true] [17592186352806 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352807 13194139535509 true] [17592186352806 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352808 13194139535509 true] [17592186352806 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352809 13194139535509 true] [175